WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.920 --> 00:00:01.753
- [Voiceover] Pop quiz.

00:00:01.753 --> 00:00:03.051
You need some info on
the Futalognkosaurus,

00:00:03.051 --> 00:00:06.349
the Baurusuchus, and the
Dicynodon, where do you go?

00:00:06.349 --> 00:00:07.182
- [Voiceover] Wikipedia.

00:00:07.182 --> 00:00:08.045
- [Voiceover] Thanks Nobumichi.

00:00:08.045 --> 00:00:09.176
- Oh no problem.

00:00:09.176 --> 00:00:10.009
- [Voiceover] How do we know what

00:00:10.009 --> 00:00:12.226
these prehistoric creatures looked like?

00:00:12.226 --> 00:00:14.764
- Lots of careful research
and attention to details.

00:00:14.764 --> 00:00:16.245
- [Voiceover] Wait, wait how do you know?

00:00:16.245 --> 00:00:17.344
- I draw them.

00:00:17.344 --> 00:00:18.294
- [Voiceover] Wait really all of them?

00:00:18.294 --> 00:00:21.044
- [Nobumichi] Well, a lot of them.

00:00:22.151 --> 00:00:24.020
- [Voiceover] This is Nobumichi Tamura.

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:24.853
- Hello.

00:00:24.853 --> 00:00:26.024
- [Voiceover] And he's
got a pretty cool day job.

00:00:26.024 --> 00:00:27.435
- I'm working at the Advance Light Source

00:00:27.435 --> 00:00:29.666
Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory.

00:00:29.666 --> 00:00:33.066
And my job here is to
take care of this machine,

00:00:33.066 --> 00:00:35.324
the microdefraction and station.

00:00:35.324 --> 00:00:36.724
- [Voiceover] The microdefrac, what?

00:00:36.724 --> 00:00:39.086
- Microdefraction and station.

00:00:39.086 --> 00:00:40.079
- [Voiceover] Ah.

00:00:40.079 --> 00:00:41.370
But it's at night and on weekends

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:43.395
that Nobu channels the scientific know-how

00:00:43.395 --> 00:00:45.042
into his dinosaur drawings.

00:00:45.042 --> 00:00:46.602
- It's called paleoart.

00:00:46.602 --> 00:00:47.661
- [Voiceover] My bad.

00:00:47.661 --> 00:00:49.812
Paleoart is an original artistic creation

00:00:49.812 --> 00:00:51.239
that depicts prehistoric life

00:00:51.239 --> 00:00:52.905
according to the latest research.

00:00:52.905 --> 00:00:53.738
Right Nobu?

00:00:53.738 --> 00:00:54.606
- Exactly.

00:00:54.606 --> 00:00:57.372
It's a combination of
science and creativity

00:00:57.372 --> 00:01:00.046
so I basically read the latest discovery

00:01:00.046 --> 00:01:03.838
on prehistoric animals
based on that factual data.

00:01:03.838 --> 00:01:06.640
This is what I've been doing
for the last eight years.

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:08.407
- [Voiceover] And Nobu's
got a lot to show for it.

00:01:08.407 --> 00:01:10.965
By his estimate he's
published over 1,500 drawings,

00:01:10.965 --> 00:01:13.245
including some in this
book that he co-authored.

00:01:13.245 --> 00:01:14.641
But it wasn't always smooth sailing.

00:01:14.641 --> 00:01:17.587
The first drawing, that
was one of a dinosaur

00:01:17.587 --> 00:01:20.203
called Chasmosaurus, but it was taken out

00:01:20.203 --> 00:01:24.689
by the editors because it
wasn't accurate enough.

00:01:24.689 --> 00:01:25.600
- [Voiceover] That's okay Nobu.

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:26.710
Can't bat a thousand.

00:01:26.710 --> 00:01:28.228
- Is that a baseball reference?

00:01:28.228 --> 00:01:29.935
I'm French.

00:01:29.935 --> 00:01:32.502
- [Voiceover] I get it not
every baguette can be perfect.

00:01:32.502 --> 00:01:33.490
- Why don't you?

00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:35.300
- [Voiceover] Wait, wait,
wait, come back, come back.

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:36.133
Come back.

